r\":2026,\"abstract\":\"Algorithmic management now shapes recruitment, scheduling, performance assessment, discipline and dismissal in workplaces far beyond the platform economy. Legal responses, however, remain divided between data protection, equality, employment, privacy and collective labour law. Using the United Kingdom as the principal case study and drawing comparative lessons from European and international instruments, this article asks what employer accountability should require when computational systems materially shape working lives. It argues that the central problem is not automation alone, but the reorganisation of authority. Algorithmic systems decide what is measured, which forms of work count, when a person attracts scrutiny and how easily an adverse judgment can be challenged. The United Kingdom has important safeguards, including rules on automated decisions, equality, privacy and unfair dismissal, but these protections do not form a coherent system of workplace governance. Transparency notices and nominal human review are particularly weak where workers lack the information, time or power to contest a decision. The article develops a Worker-Centred Algorithmic Accountability Standard built around necessity, advance explanation, equality testing, genuine human judgment, collective participation, vendor responsibility and effective remedy. The model is designed to travel across legal systems without assuming that one regulatory instrument can resolve every harm. It concludes that high-impact workplace technologies should be governed as exercises of managerial power, not merely as dataprocessing tools.\",\"doi\":\"10.2139/ssrn.7155838\",\"url\":\"https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.7155838\",\"source\":\"crossref\",\"citationCount\":0},{\"id\":\"crossref:10.5465/amproc.2024.13445abstract\",\"title\":\"How Algorithmic Management Shapes Sticky Platform-Worker Relationships\",\"authors\":[{\"name\":\"Jing Zhan\"},{\"name\":\"Meizhi Pan\"}],\"year\":2024,\"j