n":1,"step":6,"message":{"source":{"kind":"tool","callId":"call_768afb41c9f947309c431d5d"},"content":[{"type":"tool-result","toolCallId":"call_768afb41c9f947309c431d5d","content":[{"type":"text","text":"{\"ok\":true,\"project\":{\"id\":\"rp-959fd298-908f-419f-ad0d-a3d7322e5656\",\"workspaceId\":\"7c8b197f-2797-4c83-b3ef-0d01be14ada8\",\"title\":\"平台经济中的算法管理与劳动者自主性\",\"discipline\":\"社会学\",\"researchQuestion\":\"算法管理如何重塑平台劳动者的自主性？\",\"researchObject\":\"平台经济中的算法管理系统与平台劳动者（如网约车司机、外卖骑手等）的自主性状况\",\"keywords\":[\"平台经济\",\"算法管理\",\"劳动者自主性\",\"零工劳动\",\"劳动控制\"],\"publicationIntent\":\"后续学术论文写作\",\"notes\":\"2024 年由研究者发起的新课题；本轮先建立项目档案并激活适配论文写作的方法学场景。场景选择记录：本会话缺少 ncpssd_search 工具，cssci-paper 与 literature-review 激活被拒（missingTools），故激活 theoretical-paper（依赖 literature_search，已校验通过），并建立对应目标 goal-490e92e9-a020-4957-bfce-e69040fbe011；empirical-paper 亦可激活但需真实数据与可追溯分析代码，待数据就绪后考虑切换。\",\"createdAt\":1789066534426,\"updatedAt\":1789066568176}}"}],"isError":false}],"role":"user","id":"3e0ed605-554a-49bb-b2db-1f21734dcf7f"}},"failed":false},{"name":"literature_search","args":"{\"limit\":3,\"providers\":\"crossref,openalex\",\"query\":\"algorithmic management worker autonomy platform labor\"}","result":{"turn":1,"step":7,"message":{"source":{"kind":"tool","callId":"call_9fa6e2c802da451faf50d931"},"content":[{"type":"tool-result","toolCallId":"call_9fa6e2c802da451faf50d931","content":[{"type":"text","text":"{\"total\":3,\"records\":[{\"id\":\"crossref:10.5040/9798881813406.ch-8\",\"title\":\"Toward Convivial Platform Labor\",\"authors\":[],\"year\":2022,\"journal\":\"Digital Working Lives\",\"doi\":\"10.5040/9798881813406.ch-8\",\"url\":\"https://doi.org/10.5040/9798881813406.ch-8\",\"source\":\"crossref\",\"citationCount\":0},{\"id\":\"crossref:10.2139/ssrn.7155838\",\"title\":\"Algorithmic Management Beyond the Platform Economy: Worker Voice, Workplace Surveillance and Employer Accountability in the United Kingdom and Comparative Perspective\",\"authors\":[{\"name\":\"Duke Eguah\"}],\"year\":2026,\"abstract\":\"Algorithmic management now shapes recruitment, scheduling, performance assessment, discipline and dismissal in workplaces far beyond the platform economy. Legal responses, however, remain divided between data protection, equality, employment, privacy and collective labour law. Using the United Kingdom as the principal case study and drawing comparative lessons from European and international instruments, this article asks what employer accountability should require when computational systems materially shape working lives. It argues that the central problem is not automation alone, but the reorganisation of authority. Algorithmic systems decide what is measured, which forms of work count, when a person attracts scrutiny and how easily an adverse judgment can be challenged. The United Kingdom has important safeguards, including rules on automated decisions, equality, privacy and unfair dismissal, but these protections do not form a coherent system of workplace governance. Transparency notices and nominal human review are particularly weak where workers lack the information, time or power to contest a decision. The article develops a Worker-Centred Algorithmic Accountability Standard built around necessity, advance explanation, equality testing, genuine human judgment, collective participation, vendor responsibility and effective remedy. The model is designed to travel across legal systems without assuming that one regulatory instrument can resolve every harm. It concludes that high-impact workplace technologies should be governed as exercises of managerial power, not merely as dataprocessing tools.\",\"doi\":\"10.2139/ssrn.7155838\",\"url\":\"https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.7155838\",\"source\":\"crossref\",\"citationCount\":0},{\"id\":\"crossref:10.5465/amproc.2024.13445abstract\",\"title\":\"How Algorithmic Management Shapes Sticky Platform-Worker Relationships\",\"authors\":[{\"name\":\"Jing Zhan\"},{\"name\":\"Meizhi Pan\"}],\"year\":2024,\"journal\":\"Academy of Management Proceedings\",\"doi\":\"10.5465/amproc.2024.13445abstract\",\"url\":\"https://doi.org/10.5465/amproc.2024.13445abstract\",\"source\":\"crossref\",\"citationCount\":0}],\"providersUsed\":[\"crossref\",\"openalex\"]}"}],"isError":false}],"role":"user","id":"a969e3c6-3c77-4da6-8d21-5cd6a480946c"}},"failed":false},{"name":"research_project_get","args":"{}","result":{"turn":1,"step":8,"message":{"source":{"kind":"tool","callId":"call_16bbaca794c54d3f80176cff"},"content":[{"type":"tool-result","toolCallId":"call_16bbaca794c54d3f80176cff","content":[{"type":"text","text":"{\"ok\":true,\"project\":{\"id\":\"rp-959fd298-908f-419f-ad0d-a3d7322e5656\",\"workspaceId\":\"7c8b197f-2797-4c83-b3ef-0d01be14ada8\",\"title\":\"平台经济中的算法管理与劳动者自主性\",\"discipline\":\"社会学\",\"researchQuestion\":\"算法管理如何重塑平台劳动者的自主性？\",\"researchObject\":\"平台经济中的算法管理系统与平台劳动者（如网约车司机、外卖骑手等）的自主性状况\",\"keywords\":[\"平台经济\",\"算法管理\",\"劳动者自主性\",\"零工劳动\",\"劳动控制\"],\"publicationIntent\":\"后续学术论文写作\",\"notes\":\"2024 年由研究者发起的新课题；本轮先建立项目档案并激活适配论文写作的方法学场景。场景选择记录：本会话缺少 ncpssd_search 工具，cssci-paper 与 literature-review 激活被拒（missingTools），故激活 theoretical-paper（依赖 literature_search，已校验通过），并建立对应目标 goal-490e92e9-a020-4957-bfce-e69040fbe011；empirical-paper 亦可激活但需真实数据与可追溯分析代码，待数据就绪后考虑切换。\",\"createdAt\":1789066534426,\"updatedAt\":1789066568176}}"}],"isError":false}],"role":"user","id":"403eaa7d-56ab-4c82-93fc-3081b115c62b"}},"failed":false},{"name":"get_goal","args":"{}","result":{"turn":1,"step":8,"message":{"source":{"kind":"tool","callId":"call_c7ef9a66e20847d7908379a3"},"content":[{"type":"tool-result","toolCallId":"call_c7ef9a66e20847d7908379a3","content":[{"type":"text","text":"{\"goal\":{\"id\":\"goal-490e92e9-a020-4957-bfce-e69040fbe011\",\"revision\":1,\"objective\":\"完成《平台经济中的算法管理与劳动者自主性》理论论文：机制框架 + 概念澄清 + 反向检查 + 可继续发展的理论命题。\",\"phase\":\"active\",\"roundsStarted\":0,\"maxGoalRounds\":256},\"activation\":\"armed\"}"}],"isError":false}],"role":"user","id":"1e096908-68f5-4377-8088-e4ef6bd98b47"}},"failed":false},{"name":"literature_search","args":"{\"limit\":6,\"providers\":\"crossref,openalex\",\"query\":\"algorithmic management platform work control workers\"}","result":{"turn":2,"step":1,"message":{"source":{"kind":"tool","callId":"call_64196c8ba27a4bf998e6f12a"},"content":[{"type":"tool-result","toolCallId":"call_64196c8ba27a4bf998e6f12a","content":[{"type":"text","text":"{\"total\":6,\"records\":[{\"id\":\"crossref:10.2139/ssrn.5161650\",\"title\":\"House of gigs. Domestic workers, algorithmic management and the Platform Work Directive\",\"authors\":[{\"name\":\"Antonio Aloisi\"},{\"name\":\"Nastazja Potocka-Sionek\"}],\"year\":2025,\"doi\":\"10.2139/ssrn.5161650\",\"url\":\"https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.5161650\",\"source\":\"crossref\",\"citationCount\":2},{\"id\":\"crossref:10.5040/9781509990887.ch-011\",\"title\":\"House of Gigs: Domestic Workers, Algorithmic Management and the EU Platform Work Directive\",\"authors\":[{\"name\":\"Antonio Aloisi\"},{\"name\":\"Nastazja Potocka-Sionek\"}],\"year\":2026,\"journal\":\"Digital Intermediaries in Domestic and Care Work\",\"doi\":\"10.5040/9781509990887.ch-011\",\"url\":\"https://doi.org/10.5040/9781509990887.ch-011\",\"source\":\"crossref\",\"citationCount\":0},{\"id\":\"crossref:10.32629/memf.v7i2.5201\",\"title\":\"Algorithmic Control and Work Motivation in the Gig Economy: A Self-Determination Theory Approach among Chinese Food Delivery Platform Workers\",\"authors\":[{\"name\":\"Xiuzhi Li\"},{\"name\":\"Jirapong Ruanggoon\"}],\"year\":2026,\"journal\":\"Modern Economics &amp; Management Forum\",\"abstract\":\"The research objectives were 1) to examine the effect of algorithmic control on the intrinsic and extrinsic work motivations of Chinese food delivery platform workers, and 2) to explore the mediating role of the need for autonomy, competence, and relatedness in the relationship between algorithmic control and work motivation among Chinese food delivery platform workers. This study employed quantitative research methods, collecting data from a sample of 547 valid food delivery platform employees in China. Data analysis included descriptive statistics (frequency, percentage, mean, standard deviation) and inferential statistics (confirmatory factor analysis, correlation analysis, and structural equation modeling based on the Bootstrap method). The findings revealed that 1) algorithmic control had a significant negative direct impact on the intrinsic motivation of food delivery platform employees, but a significant positive direct impact on their extrinsic motivation, indicating an \\\"intrinsic-extrinsic displacement\\\" phenomenon, and 2) basic psychological needs played a complex mediating role: algorithmic control significantly inhibited intrinsic motivation by weakening the need for autonomy and relatedness, but simultaneously enhanced employees' sense of competence through clear task guidance and real-time feedback, thus supporting intrinsic motivation to some extent. Based on the findings of this study, to build a sustainable and human-centric gig economy ecosystem, the following three governance recommendations are proposed. First, reframe algorithmic design by integrating autonomy, competence, and relatedness into the core decision-making logic, transforming algorithms from \\\"control tools\\\" to \\\"empowerment partners\\\". Second, establish a hybrid governance system combining algorithms and human support through dedicated welfare officers and bidirectional negotiation mechanisms, addressing social isolation and psychological need deficits caused by algorithmic control. Third, implement a real-time monitoring and dynamic adjustment mechanism based on psychological needs, enabling platforms to shift from static monitoring to dynamic responsiveness and effectively preserve workers' intrinsic motivation.\",\"doi\":\"10.32629/memf.v7i2.5201\",\"url\":\"https://doi.org/10.32629/memf.v7i2.5201\",\"source\":\"crossref\",\"citationCount\":0},{\"id\":\"crossref:10.5772/intechopen.94524\",\"title\":\"Evaluation of Algorithmic Management of Digital Work Platform in Developing Countries\",\"authors\":[{\"name\":\"Olalekan Samuel Ogunleye\"},{\"name\":\"Billy Mathias Kalema\"}],\"year\":2021,\"journal\":\"Automation and Control\",\"abstract\":\"The economy of the Modern Work Platform is becoming increasingly relevant due to the spread of information and communication technology. As a result, digital work has gained popularity as a source of employment, especially in an economy where finding decent work is becoming increasingly difficult. Computer algorithms are now being used to alter and change the way people operate in increasing job specialization, handling large-scale human labour in a distributed manner. In these structures, human works are delegated, supplemented, and analyzed using tracked data and algorithms. Building on emerging algorithmic literature and qualitative examination, this article assesses the mechanisms by which the digital network manages staff in the sense of Uber, Bolt (formerly Taxify). It describes the difference in the degree to which such platforms limit freedoms over schedules and activities relevant to gig work. Based on in-depth interviews with 41 respondents working on different digital media and a survey of 105 staff on the same platform, the study finds that while all digital work platforms use algorithm management to delegate and assess work, substantial cross-platform variation. Uber, the largest network for ride-sharing, exercises a type of control called “algorithmic despotism” that controls the time and activities of staff more strictly than other network distribution firms. We end with a debate on the implications for the future of work of the spectrum of algorithmic power. It also addresses how algorithmic management and data-driven systems can be developed to build an improved workplace with intelligent machines, with implications for future work.\",\"doi\":\"10.5772/intechopen.94524\",\"url\":\"https://doi.org/10.5772/intechopen.94524\",\"source\":\"crossref\",\"citationCount\":0},{\"id\":\"crossref:10.5465/amproc.2025.22746abstract\",\"title\":\"Algorithmic Control in Non-Platform Organizations – Workers’ Legitimacy Judgments\",\"authors\":[{\"name\":\"Felix Hirsch\"}],\"year\":2025,\"journal\":\"Academy of Management Proceedings\",\"doi\":\"10.5465/amproc.2025.22746abstract\",\"url\":\"https://doi.org/10.5465/amproc.2025.22746abstract\",\"source\":\"crossref\",\"citationCount\":0},{\"id\":\"crossref:10.1111/ntwe.12177\",\"title\":\"When food‐delivery platform workers consent to algorithmic management: a Foucauldian perspective\",\"authors\":[{\"name\":\"Sophia Galière\"}],\"year\":2020,\"journal\":\"New Technology, Work and Employment\",\"abstract\":\"This paper extends research on algorithmic management by examining mechanisms of compliance. Algorithmic management has predominantly been analysed in terms of the exercise of disciplinary power over workers and rational control of labour. Facing algorithms, platform workers would be in a situation of fear, passivity and frustration. In this paper, we utilise the Foucauldian framework of ‘dispositive’ in order to reconceptualise platforms as exerting both rational and normative control. Based on a qualitative case study of the food‐delivery platform Deliveroo, we underscore that algorithmic rational control, although fallible, is being reinforced by techniques of subjectification. Several dispositives on the platform, such as the pay‐per‐delivery and shift picker systems, generate an active mobilisation of workers. Our discussion highlights the governmentality power of algorithmic management, which builds consent by promoting a hyper‐meritocratic ideal of justice.\",\"doi\":\"10.1111/ntwe.12177\",\"url\":\"https://doi.org/10.1111/ntwe.12177\",\"source\":\"crossref\",\"citationCount\":167}],\"providersUsed\":[\"crossref\",\"openalex\"]}"}],"isError":false}],"role":"user","id":"82019735-1716-4875-8698-6da82306abc9"}},"failed":false},{"name":"literature_search","args":"{\"limit\":6,\"providers\":\"crossref,openalex\",\"query\":\"gig economy worker autonomy discretion platform labor\"}","result":{"turn":2,"step":1,"message":{"source":{"kind":"tool","callId":"call_094fd50f027641ba8403ccac"},"content":[{"type":"tool-result","toolCallId":"call_094fd50f027641ba8403ccac","content":[{"type":"text","text":"{\"total\":6,\"records\":[{\"id\":\"crossref:10.5040/9798881813406.ch-8\",\"title\":\"Toward Convivial Platform Labor\",\"authors\":[],\"year\":2022,\"journal\":\"Digital Working Lives\",\"doi\":\"10.5040/9798881813406.ch-8\",\"url\":\"https://doi.org/10.5040/9798881813406.ch-8\",\"source\":\"crossref\",\"citationCount\":0},{\"id\":\"crossref:10.5040/9798881813406.ch-4\",\"title\":\"Alienation in the Platform Economy\",\"authors\":[],\"year\":2022,\"journal\":\"Digital Working Lives\",\"doi\":\"10.5040/9798881813406.ch-4\",\"url\":\"https://doi.org/10.5040/9798881813406.ch-4\",\"source\":\"crossref\",\"citationCount\":0},{\"id\":\"crossref:10.5040/9798881813406.ch-7\",\"title\":\"Worker Autonomy as Conviviality\",\"authors\":[],\"year\":2022,\"journal\":\"Digital Working Lives\",\"doi\":\"10.5040/9798881813406.ch-7\",\"url\":\"https://doi.org/10.5040/9798881813406.ch-7\",\"source\":\"crossref\",\"citationCount\":0},{\"id\":\"crossref:10.5771/9781538173749-135\",\"title\":\"Chapter 8 ﻿Toward Convivial Platform Labor﻿\",\"authors\":[{\"name\":\"Tim Christiaens\"}],\"year\":2022,\"journal\":\"Digital Working Lives\",\"doi\":\"10.5771/9781538173749-135\",\"url\":\"https://doi.org/10.5771/9781538173749-135\",\"source\":\"crossref\",\"citationCount\":0},{\"id\":\"crossref:10.5771/9781538173749-59\",\"title\":\"﻿Chapter 4 Alienation ﻿in﻿ ﻿the﻿ Platform Economy﻿\",\"authors\":[{\"name\":\"Tim Christiaens\"}],\"year\":2022,\"journal\":\"Digital Working Lives\",\"doi\":\"10.5771/9781538173749-59\",\"url\":\"https://doi.org/10.5771/9781538173749-59\",\"source\":\"crossref\",\"citationCount\":0},{\"id\":\"crossref:10.5040/9798881813406.ch-6\",\"title\":\"Worker Autonomy as Collective Self-Valorization?\",\"authors\":[],\"year\":2022,\"journal\":\"Digital Working Lives\",\"doi\":\"10.5040/9798881813406.ch-6\",\"url\":\"https://doi.org/10.5040/9798881813406.ch-6\",\"source\":\"crossref\",\"citationCount\":0}],\"providersUsed\":[\"crossref\",\"openalex\"]}"}],"isError":false}],"role":"user","id":"17321d6c-ee81-4bf1-aae6-d52384701cf7"}},"failed":false},{"name":"glob","args":"{\"pattern\":\"**/review-draft.md\"}","result":{"turn":2,"step":1,"message":{"source":{"kind":"tool","callId":"call_7c946f781a8942779cdd2cfb"},"content":[{"type":"tool-result","toolCallId":"call_7c946f781a8942779cdd2cfb","content":[{"type":"text","text":"review-draft.md"}],"isError":false}],"role":"user","id":"ebe8aa3e-7834-43c6-8307-73a2974e3bcc"},"meta":{"shape":"paths","paths":["review-draft.md"],"truncated":false,"total":1}},"failed":false},{"name":"read","args":"{\"file_path\":\"review-draft.md\"}","result":{"turn":2,"step":2,"message":{"source":{"kind":"tool","callId":"call_50a51c66d911456381058baf"},"content":[{"type":"tool-result","toolCallId":"call_50a51c66d911456381058baf","content":[{"type":"text","text":"<path>C:\\Users\\lauze\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\metis-real-agent-MU7zQH\\workspace\\review-draft.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # 审读报告（真实 Agent E2E 目标文件）\n\n(End of file - total 1 lines)\n</content>"}],"isError":false}],"role":"user","id":"1f310d93-7cf9-4ce0-afe0-4dd031d3c5e0"},"meta":{"path":"C:\\Users\\lauze\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\metis-real-agent-MU7zQH\\workspace\\review-draft.md","offset":1,"lines":[{"number":1,"text":"# 审读报告（真实 Agent E2E 目标文件）"}],"totalLines":1,"lang":"md"}},"failed":false},{"name":"literature_search","args":"{\"limit\":6,\"query\":\"Kellogg Valentine Christin algorithms at work contested terrain of control\"}","result":{"turn":2,"step":2,"message":{"source":{"kind":"tool","callId":"call_a96bf1a0c52f4b829ffb81d3"},"content":[{"type":"tool-result","toolCallId":"call_a96bf1a0c52f4b829ffb81d3","content":[{"type":"text","text":"{\"total\":6,\"records\":[{\"id\":\"crossref:10.5465/annals.2018.0174\",\"title\":\"Algorithms at Work: The New Contested Terrain of Control\",\"authors\":[{\"name\":\"Katherine C. Kellogg\"},{\"name\":\"Melissa A. Valentine\"},{\"name\":\"Angéle Christin\"}],\"year\":2020,\"journal\":\"Academy of Management Annals\",\"doi\":\"10.5465/annals.2018.0174\",\"url\":\"https://doi.org/10.5465/annals.2018.0174\",\"source\":\"crossref\",\"citationCount\":2161},{\"id\":\"crossref:10.23943/princeton/9780691175232.001.0001\",\"title\":\"Metrics at Work\",\"authors\":[{\"name\":\"Angele Christin\"}],\"year\":2020,\"abstract\":\"When the news moved online, journalists suddenly learned what their audiences actually liked, through algorithmic technologies that scrutinize web traffic and activity. Has this advent of audience metrics changed journalists' work practices and professional identities? This book documents the ways that journalists grapple with audience data in the form of clicks, and analyzes how new forms of clickbait journalism travel across national borders. Drawing on four years of fieldwork in web newsrooms in the United States and France, including more than one hundred interviews with journalists, the book reveals many similarities among the media groups examined—their editorial goals, technological tools, and even office furniture. Yet the book uncovers crucial and paradoxical differences in how American and French journalists understand audience analytics and how these affect the news produced in each country. American journalists routinely disregard traffic numbers and primarily rely on the opinion of their peers to define journalistic quality. Meanwhile, French journalists fixate on internet traffic and view these numbers as a sign of their resonance in the public sphere. The book offers cultural and historical explanations for these disparities, arguing that distinct journalistic traditions structure how journalists make sense of digital measurements in the two countries. Contrary to the popular belief that analytics and algorithms are globally homogenizing forces, the book shows that computational technologies can have surprisingly divergent ramifications for work and organizations worldwide.\",\"doi\":\"10.23943/princeton/9780691175232.001.0001\",\"url\":\"https://doi.org/10.23943/princeton/9780691175232.001.0001\",\"source\":\"crossref\",\"citationCount\":26},{\"id\":\"crossref:10.2307/j.ctvthhdtc\",\"title\":\"Metrics at Work\",\"authors\":[{\"name\":\"Angèle Christin\"}],\"year\":2020,\"doi\":\"10.2307/j.ctvthhdtc\",\"url\":\"https://doi.org/10.2307/j.ctvthhdtc\",\"source\":\"crossref\",\"citationCount\":8},{\"id\":\"crossref:10.23943/princeton/9780691175232.003.0008\",\"title\":\"Conclusion\",\"authors\":[{\"name\":\"Angèle Christin\"}],\"year\":2020,\"journal\":\"Metrics at Work\",\"abstract\":\"This chapter explores the implications of web analytics for further studies of digital metrics beyond the case of journalism. At a time when nearly every domain is affected by analytics and algorithms, the chapter also provides an overview of what kinds of changes are to be expected and what should not be taken for granted whenever metrics take over. It describes how online media became a different place following the election of Donald Trump as the forty-fifth president of the United States in which news organizations and digital platforms entered into a political and economic maelstrom. It investigates the moral panic surrounding the uncovering of “content farms” and the stream of tweets from the White House labelling mainstream news organizations as “fake news” that caused the media ecosystem to become the center of new controversies about the future of information and democracy. The chapter also shows how news websites can bear some responsibility for problematic developments in journalism.\",\"doi\":\"10.23943/princeton/9780691175232.003.0008\",\"url\":\"https://doi.org/10.23943/princeton/9780691175232.003.0008\",\"source\":\"crossref\",\"citationCount\":0},{\"id\":\"crossref:10.23943/princeton/9780691175232.003.0001\",\"title\":\"Introduction\",\"authors\":[{\"name\":\"Angèle Christin\"}],\"year\":2020,\"journal\":\"Metrics at Work\",\"abstract\":\"This chapter examines how the multiplication of digital metrics, analytics, and algorithms is reconfiguring work practices and professional identities. It focuses on the case of journalism, a field that has been profoundly changed by digital technologies. It describes modern newsrooms that use digital tools in the gathering, production, and diffusion of information on the web, from group chats to social media platforms and content management systems. The chapter also introduces a new market that emerged for “web analytics” or software programs that track the behaviour and preferences of internet users. It describes how editors and journalists are provided with a constant stream of data about their audience, receiving increasingly detailed information in real time about the number of visitors, comments, likes, and tweets that their articles attract.\",\"doi\":\"10.23943/princeton/9780691175232.003.0001\",\"url\":\"https://doi.org/10.23943/princeton/9780691175232.003.0001\",\"source\":\"crossref\",\"citationCount\":0},{\"id\":\"crossref:10.1515/9780691200002\",\"title\":\"Metrics at Work\",\"authors\":[{\"name\":\"Angele Christin\"}],\"year\":2020,\"doi\":\"10.1515/9780691200002\",\"url\":\"https://doi.org/10.1515/9780691200002\",\"source\":\"crossref\",\"citationCount\":21}],\"providersUsed\":[\"crossref\",\"openalex\",\"ncpssd\"]}"}],"isError":false}],"role":"user","id":"604ba61f-8768-4d19-abc6-ce7f44e0d0ba"}},"failed":false},{"name":"literature_search","args":"{\"limit\":6,\"query\":\"labor process theory consent control gig workers platform subjectivity\"}","result":{"turn":2,"step":2,"message":{"source":{"kind":"tool","callId":"call_8e089ba1b2fb4aaf811900c0"},"content":[{"type":"tool-result","toolCallId":"call_8e089ba1b2fb4aaf811900c0","content":[{"type":"text","text":"{\"total\":6,\"records\":[{\"id\":\"crossref:10.31235/osf.io/g6ydw\",\"title\":\"Consent and Contestation: How Platform Workers Reckon with the Risks of Gig Labor\",\"authors\":[{\"name\":\"Juliet Schor\"},{\"name\":\"Stephen Vallas\"},{\"name\":\"Christopher Tirrell\"}],\"year\":2023,\"abstract\":\"How do gig workers respond to the various financial, physical, and legal risks their work entails? Answers to this question have remained unclear, largely because previous studies have overlooked structurally-induced variations in the experience of platform work. In this paper we develop a theory of differential embeddedness to explain why workers’ orientations toward the risks of gig work vary. We argue further that because platforms define themselves merely as mediators of exchanges between workers and customers, they systematically expose workers to various forms of customer malfeasance, ranging from fraud and tip baiting to harassment and assault. We develop this perspective using interviews with 70 workers in the ride-hail, grocery shopping and food delivery sectors. The structure of labor platforms indirectly invites workers to exhibit distinct normative orientations toward the risks that gig work entails while also multiplying the sources of these risks.\",\"doi\":\"10.31235/osf.io/g6ydw\",\"url\":\"https://doi.org/10.31235/osf.io/g6ydw\",\"source\":\"crossref\",\"citationCount\":7},{\"id\":\"crossref:10.1007/978-981-95-1841-8_6\",\"title\":\"Labour Process and Manufacturing Consent in Platform Capitalism\",\"authors\":[{\"name\":\"Arif Novianto\"}],\"year\":2025,\"journal\":\"Cheap Labour Regime in Platform Capitalism\",\"doi\":\"10.1007/978-981-95-1841-8_6\",\"url\":\"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-981-95-1841-8_6\",\"source\":\"crossref\",\"citationCount\":0},{\"id\":\"crossref:10.2139/ssrn.5379481\",\"title\":\"Platform Labor and Financial Instability: Sectoral Insights on Gig Workers' Earnings Elasticity\",\"authors\":[{\"name\":\"Phuong Nguyen Ngoc Thanh\"}],\"year\":2025,\"doi\":\"10.2139/ssrn.5379481\",\"url\":\"https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.5379481\",\"source\":\"crossref\",\"citationCount\":0},{\"id\":\"crossref:10.2139/ssrn.5041559\",\"title\":\"Manufacturing Workers or Platform Gig Workers? The Impact of Digital Transformation in Manufacturing and Service Sectors On Job Quality and Labor Allocation\",\"authors\":[{\"name\":\"Qing Guo\"},{\"name\":\"Xuan Liu\"}],\"year\":2024,\"doi\":\"10.2139/ssrn.5041559\",\"url\":\"https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.5041559\",\"source\":\"crossref\",\"citationCount\":0},{\"id\":\"crossref:10.1177/09500170231199404\",\"title\":\"Consent and Contestation: How Platform Workers Reckon with the Risks of Gig Labor\",\"authors\":[{\"name\":\"Juliet B Schor\"},{\"name\":\"Christopher Tirrell\"},{\"name\":\"Steven Peter Vallas\"}],\"year\":2023,\"journal\":\"Work, Employment and Society\",\"abstract\":\"How do gig workers respond to the various financial, physical, and legal risks their work entails? Answers to this question have remained unclear, largely because previous studies have overlooked structurally induced variations in the experience of platform work. In this article, we develop a theory of differential embeddedness to explain why workers’ orientations toward the risks of gig work vary. We argue further that because platforms define themselves merely as mediators of exchanges between workers and customers, they systematically expose workers to various forms of customer malfeasance, ranging from fraud and tip baiting to harassment and assault. We develop this perspective using interviews with 70 workers in the ride-hail, grocery shopping, and food delivery sectors. The structure of labor platforms indirectly invites workers to exhibit distinct normative orientations toward the risks that gig work entails while also multiplying the sources of these risks.\",\"doi\":\"10.1177/09500170231199404\",\"url\":\"https://doi.org/10.1177/09500170231199404\",\"source\":\"crossref\",\"citationCount\":63},{\"id\":\"crossref:10.2139/ssrn.3383159\",\"title\":\"Regulating the Autonomy of Gig Workers. A Paternalizing Look into the Consent-Based Platform Work Economy\",\"authors\":[{\"name\":\"Marta Santos Silva\"},{\"name\":\"Mijke Houwerzijl\"}],\"year\":2019,\"journal\":\"SSRN Electronic Journal\",\"doi\":\"10.2139/ssrn.3383159\",\"url\":\"https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.3383159\",\"source\":\"crossref\",\"citationCount\":1}],\"providersUsed\":[\"crossref\",\"openalex\",\"ncpssd\"]}"}],"isError":false}],"role":"user","id":"02d0bdad-edd3-4e1c-a0cf-3ac733963e2c"}},"failed":false},{"name":"literature_get","args":"{\"doi\":\"10.5465/annals.2018.0174\"}","result":{"turn":2,"step":3,"message":{"source":{"kind":"tool","callId":"call_18f65fe7f47b4345b5b92a72"},"content":[{"type":"tool-result","toolCallId":"call_18f65fe7f47b4345b5b92a72","content":[{"type":"text","text":"{\"found\":true,\"record\":{\"id\":\"crossref:10.5465/annals.2018.0174\",\"title\":\"Algorithms at Work: The New Contested Terrain of Control\",\"authors\":[{\"name\":\"Katherine C. Kellogg\"},{\"name\":\"Melissa A. Valentine\"},{\"name\":\"Angéle Christin\"}],\"year\":2020,\"journal\":\"Academy of Management Annals\",\"doi\":\"10.5465/annals.2018.0174\",\"url\":\"https://doi.org/10.5465/annals.2018.0174\",\"source\":\"crossref\",\"citationCount\":2161}}"}],"isError":false}],"role":"user","id":"bc4216d6-34cb-4d8a-b770-8d67e4bf6a24"}},"failed":false},{"name":"literature_get","args":"{\"doi\":\"10.1111/ntwe.12177\"}","result":{"turn":2,"step":3,"message":{"source":{"kind":"tool","callId":"call_5e8c987de15f4d5b88242225"},"content":[{"type":"tool-result","toolCallId":"call_5e8c987de15f4d5b88242225","content":[{"type":"text","text":"{\"found\":true,\"record\":{\"id\":\"crossref:10.1111/ntwe.12177\",\"title\":\"When food‐delivery platform workers consent to algorithmic management: a Foucauldian perspective\",\"authors\":[{\"name\":\"Sophia Galière\"}],\"year\":2020,\"journal\":\"New Technology, Work and Employment\",\"abstract\":\"This paper extends research on algorithmic management by examining mechanisms of compliance. Algorithmic management has predominantly been analysed in terms of the exercise of disciplinary power over workers and rational control of labour. Facing algorithms, platform workers would be in a situation of fear, passivity and frustration. In this paper, we utilise the Foucauldian framework of ‘dispositive’ in order to reconceptualise platforms as exerting both rational and normative control. Based on a qualitative case study of the food‐delivery platform Deliveroo, we underscore that algorithmic rational control, although fallible, is being reinforced by techniques of subjectification. Several dispositives on the platform, such as the pay‐per‐delivery and shift picker systems, generate an active mobilisation of workers. Our discussion highlights the governmentality power of algorithmic management, which builds consent by promoting a hyper‐meritocratic ideal of justice.\",\"doi\":\"10.1111/ntwe.12177\",\"url\":\"https://doi.org/10.1111/ntwe.12177\",\"source\":\"crossref\",\"citationCount\":167}}"}],"isError":false}],"role":"user","id":"1c903cb6-d7af-42fb-a6a1-e7caf1c06009"}},"failed":false},{"name":"literature_get","args":"{\"doi\":\"10.5040/9798881813406.ch-7\"}","result":{"turn":2,"step":3,"message":{"source":{"kind":"tool","callId":"call_b739f16aea354ac5a209371e"},"content":[{"type":"tool-result","toolCallId":"call_b739f16aea354ac5a209371e","content":[{"type":"text","text":"{\"found\":true,\"record\":{\"id\":\"crossref:10.5040/9798881813406.ch-7\",\"title\":\"Worker Autonomy as Conviviality\",\"authors\":[],\"year\":2022,\"journal\":\"Digital Working Lives\",\"doi\":\"10.5040/9798881813406.ch-7\",\"url\":\"https://doi.org/10.5040/9798881813406.ch-7\",\"source\":\"crossref\",\"citationCount\":0}}"