ddedness to explain why workers' orientations toward the risks of gig work vary. We argue further that because platforms define themselves merely as mediators of exchanges between workers and customers, they systematically expose workers to various forms of customer malfeasance, ranging from fraud and tip baiting to harassment and assault. We develop this perspective using interviews with 70 workers in the ride-hail, grocery shopping, and food delivery sectors. The structure of labor platforms indirectly invites workers to exhibit distinct normative orientations toward the risks that gig work entails while also multiplying the sources of these risks.\",\"doi\":\"10.1177/09500170231199404\",\"url\":\"https://doi.org/10.1177/09500170231199404\",\"citationCount\":63,\"verificationState\":\"unverified\",\"evidenceId\":\"ev-6eff884e-b233-4293-ab47-ab618789e676\",\"projectId\":\"rp-959fd298-908f-419f-ad0d-a3d7322e5656\"},{\"id\":\"lit-5206e4f7-2fcc-4f3d-9e1b-02f02b351596\",\"title\":\"When food‐delivery platform workers consent to algorithmic management: a Foucauldian perspective\",\"authors\":[{\"name\":\"Sophia Galière\"}],\"year\":2020,\"source\":\"crossref\",\"journal\":\"New Technology, Work and Employment\",\"abstract\":\"This paper extends research on algorithmic management by examining mechanisms of compliance. Algorithmic management has predominantly been analysed in terms of the exercise of disciplinary power over workers and rational control of labour. Facing algorithms, platform workers would be in a situation of fear, passivity and frustration. In this paper, we utilise the Foucauldian framework of 'dispositive' in order to reconceptualise platforms as exerting both rational and normative control. Based on a qualitative case study of the food‐delivery platform Deliveroo, we underscore that algorithmic rational control, although fallible, is being reinforced by techniques of subjectification. Several dispositives on the platform, such as the pay‐per‐delivery and shift picker systems, generate an active mobilisation of workers. Our discussion highlights the governmentality power of algorithmic management, which builds consent by promoting a hyper‐meritocratic ideal of justice.\",\"doi\":\"10.1111/ntwe.12177\",\"url\":\"https://doi.org/10.1111/ntwe.12177\",\"citationCount\":167,\"verificationState\":\"unverified\",\"evidenceId\":\"ev-b9e3fa93-8025-4c27-afbc-a02b098cf015\",\"projectId\":\"rp-959fd298-908f-419f-ad0d-a3d7322e5656\"},{\"id\":\"lit-5d2d1d18-f96d-4168-9d29-2051fe1ff73e\",\"title\":\"Algorithms at Work: The New Contested Terrain of Control\",\"authors\":[{\"name\":\"Katherine C. Kellogg\"},{\"name\":\"Melissa A. Valentine\"},{\"name\":\"Angéle Christin\"}],\"year\":2020,\"source\":\"crossref\",\"journal\":\"Academy of Management Annals\",\"doi\":\"10.5465/annals.2018.0174\",\"url\":\"https://doi.org/10.5465/annals.2018.0174\",\"citationCount\":2161,\"verificationState\":\"unverified\",\"evidenceId\":\"ev-ba3e56a7-3d97-42d1-9d4a-6d32e0bcf08c\",\"projectId\":\"rp-959fd298-908f-419f-ad0d-a3d7322e5656\"}]}"}],"isError":false}],"role":"user","id":"e0d0327b-4ded-4120-82ff-bce5130959ac"}},"failed":false},{"name":"evidence_query","args":"{\"projectId\":\"rp-959fd298-908f-419f-ad0d-a3d7322e5656\"}","result":{"turn":2,"step":6,"message":{"source":{"kind":"tool","callId":"call_9c4882ef929c4b4e961b7c4f"},"content":[{"type":"tool-result","toolCallId":"call_9c4882ef929c4b4e961b7c4f","content":[{"type":"text","text":"{\"total\":3,\"records\":[{\"id\":\"ev-6eff884e-b233-4293-ab47-ab618789e676\",\"projectId\":\"rp-959fd298-908f-419f-ad0d-a3d7322e5656\",\"title\":\"Consent and Contestation: How Platform Workers Reckon with the Risks of Gig Labor\",\"sourceType\":\"literature\",\"provider\":\"crossref\",\"doi\":\"10.1177/09500170231199404\",\"url\":\"https://doi.org/10.1177/09500170231199404\",\"verificationState\":\"unverified\",\"observedAt\":1789066707192},{\"id\":\"ev-b9e3fa93-8025-4c27-afbc-a02b098cf015\",\"projectId\":\"rp-95